    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-what">
      <h2>What Is an Hourglass Body Shape?</h2>
      <p>An hourglass body shape has a bust and hips that are close to equal in width, with a waist that is clearly, visibly narrower than both. It's the shape most people picture when they hear "curvy" — but the definition is stricter than that word suggests. Not to know your exact shape yet? Run your numbers through our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> first; the rest of this guide will make more sense once you have your own figures in front of you.</p>
      <p>In measurement terms, the difference between your bust and hips has to be under 3.6 inches, and your waist has to sit at least 9 inches smaller than your bust <em>or</em> at least 10 inches smaller than your hips. Both conditions matter — a body with balanced bust and hips but a soft, undefined waist is a rectangle, not an hourglass. The waist is what earns the name.</p>
      <div class="hbs-note">
        <p><strong>Quick check:</strong> bust no more than 1 inch bigger than hips, hips no more than 3.6 inches bigger than bust, waist 9+ inches smaller than the bust. If that's you, you're a true hourglass — not top hourglass, not bottom hourglass, the classic version.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="hbs-fig">
        <img src="/assets/images/hourglass-body-shape-3d-front-side-back.webp" width="1500" height="1136" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Hourglass body shape 3D illustration from the front, side and back, with bust, waist and hips measurement lines labelled at 38, 28 and 39 inches">
        <figcaption class="hbs-figcap">An hourglass body shape rendered in 3D from the measurements above: bust and hips balanced, waist about 10 in smaller. This is the same engine that powers our <a href="/body-visualizer/">body visualizer</a> — open it to rotate the figure yourself and try your own numbers.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>Prefer to see it interactively? The <a href="/body-visualizer/">3D body visualizer</a> has an <strong>Hourglass</strong> preset: pick the female model, tap the chip and rotate the figure to see how the bust, waist and hip balance looks from every angle. The Top hourglass and Bottom hourglass presets sit right beside it for comparison.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-rare">
      <h2>How Rare Is the Hourglass Shape, Really?</h2>
      <p>The most-cited figure comes from a 2006 North Carolina State University analysis of more than 6,300 women, led by Dr. Cindy Istook, which found that only about 8% measured as a true hourglass — Rectangle came out far more common at roughly 46%, followed by pear at around 20% and apple at around 14%. That specific breakdown traces to a press release rather than a published table, and a 2021 re-analysis of the same formulas on 3D body scans found a notably higher hourglass share (26–34%) — so treat these as the most commonly repeated figures, not an exact census. The classification <em>formula</em> itself is separately documented in peer-reviewed clothing-science literature.</p>
      <p>That 8% figure surprises most people, because "hourglass" gets used loosely in everyday conversation to describe anyone with visible curves. The strict version — where bust and hips are within an inch of each other <em>and</em> the waist is dramatically smaller than both — is the rarest of the seven shapes, not the default "curvy" category.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-compare">
      <h2>Hourglass vs Top Hourglass vs Bottom Hourglass</h2>
      <p>These three get confused constantly because they share the same defined waist. The difference is which half is slightly larger — and it changes what actually flatters you.</p>
      <div class="hbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trait</th><th scope="col">Hourglass</th><th scope="col">Top Hourglass</th><th scope="col">Bottom Hourglass</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bust vs. hips</td><td>Within 1 in of each other</td><td>Bust 1–10 in larger</td><td>Hips 3.6–10 in larger</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waist</td><td>≥9 in smaller than bust</td><td>≥9 in smaller than bust</td><td>≥9 in smaller than hips</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visual balance</td><td>Symmetrical</td><td>Fuller on top</td><td>Fuller below</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Styling focus</td><td>Show the waist, either half works</td><td>Necklines and tops carry the look</td><td>Bottoms and hemlines carry the look</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="hbs-fig">
        <img src="/assets/images/hourglass-vs-top-vs-bottom-hourglass-3d.webp" width="1500" height="1148" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of hourglass, top hourglass and bottom hourglass body shapes at the same hip size, with bust, waist and hips labelled">
        <figcaption class="hbs-figcap">Same hip size, three different balances — notice how little the waist itself moves compared to the bust.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>If you've tried classic hourglass styling advice and it hasn't quite landed, this table is usually why — you're likely a top or bottom hourglass, and the small shift in emphasis (upper body vs. lower body) makes a real difference in what looks intentional versus slightly off. Our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> distinguishes all three using your exact numbers.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-whr">
      <h2>What's a Healthy Waist-to-Hip Ratio for This Shape?</h2>
      <p>Because the hourglass definition requires a waist well below the bust and hip measurements, the waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) for this shape usually lands between 0.65 and 0.75 — comfortably under the WHO's 0.85 threshold for elevated health risk in women. A defined waist and a low WHR tend to go together, though WHR is worth checking on its own rather than assumed from shape alone, since it uses your hip measurement specifically rather than bust.</p>
      <p>Get the exact number and what it means for you on our <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist to hip ratio calculator</a> — it takes the same two measurements you've likely already got on hand. Our <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a> adds your bust-to-waist ratio and leg length to the same numbers.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-style">
      <h2>How to Dress for an Hourglass Body Shape</h2>
      <p>The core rule for this shape is simple and easy to forget: your proportions are already balanced, so clothing that <em>hides</em> the waist is working against you, not for you. The sections below work through each category piece by piece — what to look for and what tends to work against you — each one shown as a 3D “avoid vs choose” pair on the same hourglass figure (38–28–39 in), so the only thing that changes between the two is the cut.</p>

      <h3 id="hourglass-necklines">Necklines</h3>
      <p>Your shoulders and hips are already balanced, so the only job a neckline has here is to not get in the way. <strong>Scoop and V-necklines</strong> follow the body’s natural line without adding width anywhere it isn’t already there — both are safe, flattering defaults. A <strong>sweetheart</strong> neckline works the same way while also echoing the curve of your waist, which is why it turns up so often in fitted dresses. What to leave alone: wide boat necklines and structured high necklines, which broaden the shoulder line for no reason when your proportions don’t need the help.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-necklines-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of necklines on an hourglass body shape — avoid: boat neckline (pulls the eye straight across the shoulders); choose: v-neck or sweetheart (opens the chest and echoes the waist curve)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hourglass-sleeves">Sleeves</h3>
      <p>The best sleeve for an hourglass shape is simply one that doesn’t compete with the waist for attention. <strong>Fitted and tailored sleeves</strong> follow the natural line of the arm and let the eye travel straight down to where your shape actually narrows. A <strong>three-quarter sleeve</strong> does the same job while showing a little more skin at the wrist. Avoid embellishment or volume at the elbow and cuff specifically — wide, dolman-style sleeves add bulk right at torso height, which visually competes with your waist instead of framing it.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-sleeves-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of sleeves on an hourglass body shape — avoid: dolman / batwing sleeve (fabric at arm level blurs the waistline); choose: fitted three-quarter sleeve (keeps a clean gap between arm and waist)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hourglass-shirts-t-shirts-and-tops">Shirts, T-Shirts &amp; Tops</h3>
      <p>Tops for this shape should <strong>follow the natural silhouette</strong> rather than fight it. A fitted or semi-fitted cut that skims the ribcage and nips in at the waist is doing exactly what it should — it doesn’t need to be tight, just shaped. A <strong>wrap top</strong> is a reliable go-to because the tie does the waist-defining work for you, no fitting required. Where this goes wrong is volume: boxy, straight-cut, or heavily oversized tops remove the waistline from the picture entirely, which is the one thing this shape doesn’t need to lose.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-tops-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of tops on an hourglass body shape — avoid: boxy, straight-cut top (hangs from the bust and hides the waist); choose: wrap top (the tie sits right at your narrowest point)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hourglass-knitwear">Knitwear</h3>
      <p>Knitwear is where an hourglass shape most often gets accidentally hidden, because knit fabric adds visual bulk even when it isn’t tight. <strong>Lightweight, fine-gauge sweaters</strong> that follow the body — rather than standing away from it — keep the waist visible under the layer. A fitted, ribbed knit or a wrap cardigan belted at the waist both do this well. Chunky, cable-knit, or heavily oversized sweaters are the ones to be careful with: they can add several visual inches to the torso and erase the waist definition that’s otherwise your strongest asset.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-knitwear-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of knitwear on an hourglass body shape — avoid: chunky, oversized knit (adds inches to the torso and erases the waist); choose: fine-gauge fitted knit (skims the body so the curve stays visible)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hourglass-jackets-and-blazers">Jackets &amp; Blazers</h3>
      <p>A jacket only flatters this shape if it acknowledges the waist rather than skipping over it. <strong>Structured, single-breasted blazers</strong> that are tailored or belted at the waist mirror your own proportions and tend to look intentional rather than borrowed. A wrap coat with a tie belt works the same way for outerwear. Boxy, straight-cut, or oversized jackets are the main thing to watch — they sit away from the body and hide the waistline you’d otherwise be showing off, which is usually the opposite of what you want from a jacket in the first place.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-jackets-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jackets on an hourglass body shape — avoid: boxy, unstructured jacket (sits away from the body at the waist); choose: tailored or belted blazer (nips in exactly where you already taper)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="hourglass-coats">Coats</h3>
      <p>Long outerwear is where an hourglass most often disappears, because a coat covers the waist and the hip at the same time. A <strong>belted trench or wrap coat</strong> solves that in one move: the belt lands at your narrowest point and the skirt of the coat flares away over the hip, so the outline underneath still reads through a heavy fabric. A single-breasted coat with shaped side seams does the same job without a belt. The one to be careful with is the cocoon or oversized straight coat — a single rounded volume from shoulder to knee that makes a 10-inch waist gap invisible, however relaxed it looks on the hanger.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-coats-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of coats on an hourglass body shape — avoid: cocoon / oversized coat (one rounded volume from shoulder to knee); choose: belted trench or wrap coat (belt at the waist, skirt of the coat flares away)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hourglass-trousers">Trousers</h3>
      <p>Because your waist is genuinely narrower than your bust and hips, <strong>high-rise trousers and jeans</strong> are worth prioritising — the waistband sits exactly where your body already narrows, so it reads as tailored rather than tight. Straight-leg and slightly bootcut styles keep the line clean from hip to hem without adding competing volume. Low-rise cuts are the one to be cautious with: they sit below your natural waistline, which means the waistband misses your narrowest point entirely and the trouser reads flatter and less shaped than it would an inch or two higher.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-trousers-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of trousers on an hourglass body shape — avoid: low-rise trousers (waistband misses your narrowest point); choose: high-rise wide-leg (sits at the natural waist, balances the hips)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="hourglass-jeans">Jeans</h3>
      <p>Denim follows the same rise rule as trousers, with one extra lever: the hem. <strong>High-rise bootcut or straight jeans</strong> sit at the waist and open very slightly below the knee, echoing the hip curve instead of tapering away from it. Because your waist is so much smaller than your hips, a gap at the back waistband is the most common fit problem — a contoured waistband or 1–2% stretch in the denim keeps it flat. Low-rise, baggy and boyfriend cuts are the ones to skip: they add fabric at the hip and drop the waistband below the point that defines your shape.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-jeans-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jeans on an hourglass body shape — avoid: low-rise baggy jeans (extra fabric at the hip, waist disappears); choose: high-rise bootcut jeans (slight flare at the hem mirrors the hip curve)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hourglass-skirts">Skirts</h3>
      <p>A skirt that follows your actual hip line will always look more deliberate on this shape than one that adds volume you don’t need to balance. A <strong>pencil skirt</strong>, cut close through the hip and nipped at the waist, is the clearest way to show the proportions you already have. A fitted A-line does the same job with slightly more ease through the thigh. Where this can go wrong is with a lot of extra volume — a full or circle skirt isn’t balancing anything on an hourglass frame, since there’s no width imbalance to correct, so it mostly just hides the waist-hip line underneath it.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-skirts-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of skirts on an hourglass body shape — avoid: full circle skirt (volume below that balances nothing); choose: pencil skirt (follows the hip line you already have)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hourglass-dresses">Dresses</h3>
      <p>A dress is the single easiest place to see why this shape earns its name — and the easiest place to lose it entirely with the wrong cut. A <strong>wrap dress</strong> is close to foolproof: the tie sits at your natural waist and the fabric follows your line on both sides of it. A fitted sheath does the same job with a more structured finish. The one silhouette worth avoiding is the <strong>shift or straight-cut dress</strong> — it runs in a single unbroken line from shoulder to hem, which on this shape means removing the waist definition that every other silhouette here is designed to show off.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-dresses-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of dresses on an hourglass body shape — avoid: shift / straight-cut dress (falls straight from the bust and erases the waist); choose: wrap dress (ties at the natural waist, follows the curve both ways)">
      </figure>

      <h3 id="hourglass-shorts">Shorts</h3>
      <p>The same logic that applies to trousers applies here: a waistband that sits at your natural waist will always look more shaped than one that sits below it. <strong>Tailored, high-waisted shorts</strong> with a fitted waist and a clean leg line keep the hourglass proportions visible even in a much shorter length. A slightly wider leg is fine as long as the waist itself stays defined. Loose, low-slung, or heavily pocketed cargo shorts are the ones to skip — the extra fabric at the hip and thigh adds bulk without any waist shaping to balance it, so the whole silhouette reads boxier than it needs to.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-shorts-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of shorts on an hourglass body shape — avoid: low-slung baggy shorts (bulk at the hip with no waist shaping); choose: tailored high-waisted shorts (keeps the waist-to-hip line intact)">
      </figure>
      <h3 id="hourglass-jumpsuits-and-playsuits">Jumpsuits &amp; Playsuits</h3>
      <p>A jumpsuit is one continuous piece of fabric from neckline to ankle, so without a waist marker it turns an hourglass into a column. Look for a <strong>belt, tie or seam at the natural waist</strong>, then let a wide or straight leg balance the hip below it. A wrap-front bodice gives you the V-neck and the waist definition in a single garment. Playsuits follow the same rule in a shorter length. The one to leave on the rail is the boxy utility jumpsuit worn without a belt — easy to wear, but it removes the one feature this shape is named after.</p>
      <figure class="hbs-gfig">
        <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/hourglass-jumpsuits-3d.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D comparison of jumpsuits on a hourglass body shape — avoid: boxy utility jumpsuit (straight torso turns the shape into a column); choose: belted wide-leg jumpsuit (belt at the waist, wide leg balances the hip)">
      </figure>

    </section>

